  <!-- PART 1: STATUTORY -->
  
<div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><div class="rule-arr-l"></div><div class="rule-line"></div><div class="rule-arr-r"></div></div>
<section id="statutory">
    <div class="section-label">Part 1 — Employee Benefits</div>
    <h2>Mandatory statutory insurance &amp; benefits</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Indian labour law mandates several contributory schemes. Thresholds are headcount and salary based. Non-compliance risks fines up to ₹1 lakh, criminal liability, and operational disruption.</p>

    <div class="callout callout-coral">
      <span class="callout-icon">⚑</span>
      <div><strong>Labour Code 2025 update:</strong> The four new Labour Codes became effective November 21, 2025, consolidating 29 prior laws. Basic salary must now be at least 50% of total CTC. Gratuity eligibility for fixed-term employees reduced from 5 years to 1 year of service.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll"><table class="statutory-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th>Scheme</th>
          <th>Who it applies to</th>
          <th>Employer contribution</th>
          <th>Employee contribution</th>
          <th>What it covers</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>EPF</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Employees' Provident Fund</span></td>
          <td>Establishments with 20+ employees. Employees earning basic+DA up to ₹15,000/month</td>
          <td>12% of basic+DA (8.33% → EPS, balance → EPF)</td>
          <td>12% of basic+DA</td>
          <td>Retirement savings, withdrawable for housing/medical</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>ESI</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Employees' State Insurance</span></td>
          <td>Establishments with 10+ employees. Employees earning ≤₹21,000/month gross</td>
          <td>3.25% of gross wages</td>
          <td>0.75% of gross wages</td>
          <td>Medical care, sickness cash benefit, maternity, occupational injury. <em>Note: ESI replaces employer obligation for group health for in-scope employees.</em></td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Gratuity</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Payment of Gratuity Act, 1972</span></td>
          <td>Establishments with 10+ employees. Employee eligible after 5 continuous years (1 year for fixed-term post-2025 reforms)</td>
          <td>15 days' last drawn salary × years of service. Capped at ₹20 lakh</td>
          <td>Nil</td>
          <td>Lump-sum on resignation, retirement, death, or disability</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Workmen's Compensation / EC</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Employees' Compensation Act, 1923</span></td>
          <td>All employees not covered by ESI — typically those earning above the ESI threshold or in non-ESI-notified areas</td>
          <td>Employer bears full cost of insurance premium</td>
          <td>Nil</td>
          <td>Compensation for occupational injury, partial/permanent disability, death in course of employment</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>EDLI</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Employees' Deposit Linked Insurance</span></td>
          <td>All EPF-covered employees (administered via EPFO)</td>
          <td>0.5% of basic wages (capped at ₹75/month per employee)</td>
          <td>Nil</td>
          <td>Life insurance cover of up to ₹7 lakh payable to nominee on death in service</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Maternity benefit</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">Maternity Benefit Act, 1961</span></td>
          <td>Establishments with 10+ employees. Women employees with 80+ days of work in past 12 months</td>
          <td>Full pay for 26 weeks (first two children); 12 weeks for third child onwards</td>
          <td>Nil (ESI-covered employees receive benefit via ESIC instead)</td>
          <td>Paid maternity leave; nursing breaks; crèche obligation for 50+ employee establishments</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
  </section>


  <!-- PART 2: VOLUNTARY EB -->
  
<div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><div class="rule-arr-l"></div><div class="rule-line"></div><div class="rule-arr-r"></div></div>
<section id="voluntary-eb">
    <div class="section-label">Part 2 — Voluntary Employee Benefits</div>
    <h2>Recommended EB insurance for MNCs</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Not legally mandatory, but market-standard for any MNC or GCC hiring professional talent in India. These benefits are a primary lever for retention — and candidates from IT, BFSI, and consulting sectors arrive with clear expectations about what a credible employer carries.</p>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon teal">🏥</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Group Health Insurance (GHI)</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Floater or individual sum insured; employee + dependants</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges">
          <span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span>
          <span class="badge badge-gc">GCC standard</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Who needs it</label>
          <p>Any employer not relying solely on ESI. Virtually mandatory for employees above the ₹21,000/month ESI threshold — which covers most MNC white-collar hires.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Minimum group size</label>
          <p>7 employees to qualify for a standard group policy. Below this, insurers require individual retail policies (30–50% higher premium, with waiting periods).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Typical coverage</label>
          <p>Hospitalisation (inpatient), pre-existing conditions covered from day one, maternity, daycare procedures, cashless claim at insurer's network hospitals. Family floater commonly covers spouse + 2 children; parents can be added as top-up.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Market benchmarks (2025–26)</label>
          <p>India median sum insured: ₹5,00,000. Global startups operating in India: ₹10,00,000. Top 10 percentile companies: ₹8,00,000 and above, regardless of headcount.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Key IRDAI 2024–25 changes</label>
          <p>Pre-existing condition waiting period capped at 36 months (down from 48). Moratorium period reduced from 8 to 5 years. No upper age limit for enrolment in new policies.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Tax treatment</label>
          <p>Premium deductible for employer under Section 37(1) of the Income Tax Act. 18% GST applies on group policies. Not included in employee's perquisite income under Section 17(2).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field full">
          <label>Insurer options</label>
          <p>
            <span class="trigger-tag">ICICI Lombard</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">HDFC ERGO</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">Bajaj Allianz</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">Star Health</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">Niva Bupa</span>
            <span class="trigger-tag">Aditya Birla Health Insurance</span>
          </p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon coral">🛡️</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Group Personal Accident (GPA)</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Accidental death, disability, income replacement</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges"><span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Accidental death (typically 5–10× annual salary), permanent total disability, permanent partial disability. Often includes weekly compensation for temporary disablement and medical expense reimbursement.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Relation to WC/EC</label>
          <p>GPA provides broader cover than mandatory Workmen's Compensation — covers non-occupational accidents and is not restricted to the workplace. MNCs typically hold both.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field full">
          <label>Why MNCs carry this</label>
          <p>EDLI under EPF provides a maximum of ₹7 lakh death benefit. GPA fills the gap for senior employees and those with global pay structures where EDLI cover is structurally insufficient. At the 90th percentile, companies offer GPA of ₹50,00,000 or higher / 3–5× CTC.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon blue">💼</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Group Term Life (GTL)</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Death benefit; employer-paid life cover</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges">
          <span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span>
          <span class="badge badge-gc">GCC standard</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Typical structure</label>
          <p>Death benefit of 3–5× annual CTC. No medical underwriting for group of sufficient size. Often extended to include critical illness and accidental death rider.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Adoption by headcount</label>
          <p>Offered by 36% of 2–100 employee companies; 52% at 101–250; 75% at 251–750; 76% at 750+. Global startups in India: 30% adoption — lagging their GHI provision.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon amber">🩺</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">OPD / Wellness Benefits</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Outpatient, mental health, preventive care</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges"><span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it includes</label>
          <p>Outpatient consultations, diagnostics, pharmacy reimbursements. Mental health support (EAP), telemedicine access, preventive health check-ups.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Adoption context</label>
          <p>Only 9% of companies at 2–100 employees offer OPD; 14% at 101–250; 35% at 251–750; 60% at 750+. Global startups in India: 60% adoption — well above the India median of 16%.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field full">
          <label>Why it matters beyond hospitalisation</label>
          <p>Group health insurance primarily covers inpatient events — but Plum's telehealth data shows mental health (19% of consultations), dermatology (18%), and OB-GYN (10%) as the top day-to-day needs. These are overwhelmingly OPD interactions that a standard GHI policy does not address.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- PART 2B: GLOBAL BENCHMARK -->
  
<div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><div class="rule-arr-l"></div><div class="rule-line"></div><div class="rule-arr-r"></div></div>
<section id="global-benchmark">
    <div class="section-label">Part 2B — Global Standard</div>
    <h2>What global MNC standard actually means, dimension by dimension</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Data from Plum's analysis of 4,500+ employee healthcare plans. "Global startups in India" are international companies that have set up operations in India and bring global benefit benchmarks with them. This is the cohort your India talent pool will increasingly compare you against.</p>

    <div class="callout callout-blue">
      <span class="callout-icon">ℹ</span>
      <div><strong>The budget gap is smaller than it looks:</strong> India's benefits budget is typically 2% of total CTC, compared to 7% in the USA, 10–25% in Europe, and 8–15% in other APAC countries. Closing the EB gap does not require closing the budget gap — it requires better plan design and broker selection.</div>
    </div>

    <div class="table-scroll"><table class="data-table">
      <thead>
        <tr>
          <th class="col-dim">Dimension</th>
          <th class="col-india">India Inc (median)</th>
          <th class="col-global">Global startups in India</th>
        </tr>
      </thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Median sum insured</strong></td>
          <td class="col-india">₹5,00,000</td>
          <td class="col-global">₹10,00,000</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Policy structure</strong></td>
          <td class="col-india">Employee, Spouse, Child</td>
          <td class="col-global">Employee, Spouse, Child, Parents</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Maternity benefits</strong></td>
          <td class="col-india">60% of companies offer it; avg cover ₹60,000</td>
          <td class="col-global">95% of companies offer it; avg cover ₹1,00,000</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>OPD &amp; pharmacy</strong></td>
          <td class="col-india">16% of companies offer it</td>
          <td class="col-global">60% of companies offer it</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Modern treatments</strong></td>
          <td class="col-india">70% include them</td>
          <td class="col-global">95% include them</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Inclusive benefits</strong><br><span style="font-size:11px;color:var(--ink-muted)">LGBTQ+, gender affirmation, infertility</span></td>
          <td class="col-india">&lt;10% of companies</td>
          <td class="col-global">80% of companies</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Employer-sponsored primary healthcare</strong></td>
          <td class="col-india">18% of companies</td>
          <td class="col-global">80% of companies</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Group personal accident</strong></td>
          <td class="col-india">45% of companies</td>
          <td class="col-global">60% of companies</td>
        </tr>
        <tr>
          <td><strong>Group term life</strong></td>
          <td class="col-india">14% of companies</td>
          <td class="col-global">30% of companies</td>
        </tr>
      </tbody>
    </table></div>
    <p style="font-size:12px;color:var(--ink-muted);margin-top:8px;">Source: Plum State of Employee Benefits 2024. Analysis of 4,500+ employee healthcare plans.</p>

    <div style="margin-top:28px;">
      <h2 style="margin-bottom:10px;">Benefits adoption by headcount band</h2>
      <p class="section-intro">How the market actually looks at each stage — so you know what your candidates have been offered by previous employers.</p>

      <div class="table-scroll"><table class="bench-table">
        <thead>
          <tr>
            <th>Benefit</th>
            <th class="b1">2–100 employees</th>
            <th class="b2">101–250 employees</th>
            <th class="b3">251–750 employees</th>
            <th class="b4">750+ employees</th>
          </tr>
        </thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr>
