  <!-- PART 3: BUSINESS INSURANCE -->
  
<div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><div class="rule-arr-l"></div><div class="rule-line"></div><div class="rule-arr-r"></div></div>
<section id="business">
    <div class="section-label">Part 3 — Business Insurance (Non-EB)</div>
    <h2>Commercial &amp; liability insurance</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Separate from the EB stack. These protect the MNC entity, its directors, its property, and its third-party obligations. Several are contractually required by clients, landlords, or parent-company risk frameworks before India operations begin.</p>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon purple">⚖️</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Directors &amp; Officers Liability (D&amp;O)</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Personal liability protection for leadership</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges">
          <span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span>
          <span class="badge badge-gc">GCC standard</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Defense costs, settlements, and judgments arising from claims by employees, shareholders, creditors, or regulators alleging wrongful acts — mismanagement, breach of duty, employment practices violations.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Indian regulatory basis</label>
          <p>Under the Companies Act 2013, there is no bar on companies indemnifying directors and officers. Premium paid is not remuneration unless the director is proven guilty of negligence or misfeasance.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Key exclusions</label>
          <p>Fraud, wilful criminal acts, fines and penalties, anti-competitive behaviour, bodily injury to employees (covered under WC), professional errors by the entity (covered under PI).</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Why MNCs buy early</label>
          <p>India-incorporated subsidiary directors are personally exposed under the Companies Act from the CIN date. MNCs often extend a global D&O policy to India via a difference-in-conditions (DIC) local placement.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon teal">📋</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Professional Indemnity (PI) / E&amp;O</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Errors, omissions, professional negligence</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges">
          <span class="badge badge-sector">Sector-specific</span>
          <span class="badge badge-gc">GCC standard</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Claims arising from professional negligence, errors, or omissions in service delivery. Covers legal defense costs, settlements, and damages. Also typically covers intellectual property infringement and unintentional defamation.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Critical distinction</label>
          <p>CGL (below) explicitly excludes professional advice and service failures. PI fills that gap. IT services, consulting, BPO, financial services, legal, and engineering firms all require PI — CGL alone is insufficient.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field full">
          <label>Who mandates it</label>
          <p>Client contracts (especially Fortune 500 or public sector), RBI for regulated entities, SEBI for market intermediaries, and sectoral licensing requirements under IRDAI for insurance intermediaries.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon coral">🏢</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Commercial General Liability (CGL)</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Third-party bodily injury &amp; property damage</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges"><span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Third-party bodily injury on premises, property damage caused by the business, product liability (for manufacturers), completed operations liability.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Key exclusions</label>
          <p>Does NOT cover employee injuries (requires WC/EC) or professional advice errors (requires PI). CGL is always paired with WC cover for complete protection.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Trigger for MNCs</label>
          <p>MNC clients routinely require a valid CGL certificate before vendor or supplier onboarding. Leased office space landlords may also require it as a condition of the lease.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Insurer ICR guidance</label>
          <p>IRDAI's target ICR: 70–90%. Avoid insurers with ICR above 95% (claims management risk) or below 50% (potentially underpaying claims).</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon blue">🔐</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Cyber Liability Insurance</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Data breaches, ransomware, regulatory fines</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges">
          <span class="badge badge-recommended">Recommended</span>
          <span class="badge badge-gc">GCC standard</span>
        </div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Data breach response costs, forensic investigation, notification to affected parties, regulatory fines under DPDP Act 2023 / IT Act, business interruption from cyber incidents, ransomware payments (subject to conditions), third-party liability from data leaks.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Regulatory trigger</label>
          <p>India's DPDP Act 2023 creates significant financial exposure for data fiduciaries. Average cost of a data breach in India: ₹195 million (IBM, 2024). MNCs processing employee or customer data in India need standalone cyber cover — CGL does not cover cyber events.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field full">
          <label>GCC / IT context</label>
          <p>43–46% of businesses in India faced at least one cyberattack in 2024. For GCCs processing global data flows, parent-company cyber programs may not extend to India operations; a local policy or endorsed global coverage is essential.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon amber">🏗️</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Property / Fire &amp; Allied Perils</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Physical assets, office contents, equipment</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges"><span class="badge badge-mandatory">Often contractual</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>What it covers</label>
          <p>Building structure (if owned), office contents, IT equipment, furniture, stock. Allied perils include earthquake, flood, storm, riot, and business interruption.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Regulatory note</label>
          <p>Not a standalone statutory requirement for most sectors, but lenders, landlords, and lease agreements typically make this a condition precedent. Standard Market Fire Policy (SMF) is the base form; "all risks" endorsements available.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="policy-card">
      <div class="policy-card-head">
        <div class="policy-icon purple">🔍</div>
        <div class="policy-title-block">
          <div class="policy-name">Crime / Fidelity &amp; Trade Credit</div>
          <div class="policy-tagline">Internal fraud, employee dishonesty, credit risk</div>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-badges"><span class="badge badge-sector">Sector-specific</span></div>
      </div>
      <div class="policy-divider"></div>
      <div class="policy-body">
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Crime / Fidelity covers</label>
          <p>Employee theft, fraud, forgery, and computer crime. Mandatory for BFSI-sector MNCs under RBI operational risk guidelines. Recommended for any entity with cash handling, treasury functions, or access to customer funds.</p>
        </div>
        <div class="policy-field">
          <label>Trade credit covers</label>
          <p>Default by B2B buyers on invoiced receivables. Relevant for MNCs with large Indian distribution networks or credit-extended channel partners.</p>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>


  <!-- PART 4: BY SETUP STAGE -->
  
<div class="rule-section" aria-hidden="true"><div class="rule-arr-l"></div><div class="rule-line"></div><div class="rule-arr-r"></div></div>
<section id="by-setup">
    <div class="section-label">Part 4 — Setup Stage</div>
    <h2>What to buy, and when</h2>
    <p class="section-intro">Insurance sequencing follows your entity and headcount milestones. Buying out of sequence creates coverage gaps and compliance risk.</p>

    <div class="checklist-grid">
      <div class="checklist-block">
        <h3><span class="phase-dot p1"></span> Phase 1 — Entity registration (Day 1–90)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>D&amp;O Liability — directors are exposed from CIN date</li>
          <li>Professional Indemnity — if client contracts already signed</li>
          <li>CGL — required by landlord or client before operations commence</li>
          <li>Cyber Liability — if any data processing begins</li>
          <li>Property / Fire — required by lease agreement</li>
          <li>Workmen's Compensation — any contract / blue-collar workers on-site</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="checklist-block">
        <h3><span class="phase-dot p2"></span> Phase 2 — First employees hired (7–19 employees)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>Group Health Insurance — minimum 7 for group policy eligibility</li>
          <li>Group Personal Accident — recommended from first hire; only 36% of small companies carry it, but GCCs are expected to</li>
          <li>Group Term Life — standard with GHI for white-collar hiring</li>
          <li>WC / EC mandatory for employees above ESI threshold</li>
          <li>EDLI auto-enrolls via EPFO when EPF registration triggers</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <div class="checklist-block">
        <h3><span class="phase-dot p3"></span> Phase 3 — Scale (20+ employees)</h3>
        <ul>
          <li>EPF mandatory — register with EPFO within 30 days of crossing 20</li>
          <li>ESI mandatory — register with ESIC within 15 days of crossing 10</li>
          <li>Gratuity fund or LIC gratuity scheme for provisioning</li>
          <li>Crèche facility required at 50+ employees (Maternity Benefit Act)</li>
          <li>OPD / wellness — 35% adoption at 251–750; competitive expectation from ~100 employees in GCC context</li>
          <li>Review GHI sum insured: 90th percentile target is ₹10L at any headcount</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="callout callout-amber" style="margin-top:20px;">
      <span class="callout-icon">⚠</span>
      <div><strong>EOR / PEO route:</strong> If setting up via an Employer of Record before entity incorporation, group insurance can be provided through the EOR's master policy, bypassing minimum group size thresholds. Confirm that the EOR's policy explicitly names the MNC and covers all in-scope employees.</div>
    </div>
  </section>


